# Cell readme;1{doc}
Creadme;1{doc}||artwork|1172013949228|1174926456122||FACET_message()S[Version 1.5,"26 March, 2007",J.Gries,Changes:,Addition of P/N-Select regions to Mux2-dp cells in order to eliminate notch errors when joining the cells with neighbors,"",Version 1.4,"14 March, 2007","J.Gries, D. Money Harris",Changes/Corrections:,"PMOS transistors in cambit changed to \"weak\"",NCC annotation added to srambit and cambit layouts denoting ground connection via parent.  This does not apparently correct the issue of NCC failure.,Minor icon changes,"","",Version 1.3,13 March 2007,J.Gries,Changes:,"PMOS transistors in srambit changed to \"weak\" for proper verilog simulation","","",Version 1.2,12 March 2007,J. Gries,Fixed:,export issues regarding invbuf_4x,minor layout problems with or3 cells (export not on column),schematic-layout discrepancies involving nand3 cells (transistor sizes),inv cell output exports were not accessible on m2 vias.,"","",Version 1.1,2 March 2007,J. Gries and D. Money Harris,correct export direction on mux,set NCC properties to connect gnd and gnd_2 in parent cell so that srambit and cambit pass NCC,minor layout tweaking,or2 schematic issues (dangling arcs),"",MuddLib 1.0,20 February 2007,"","The VLSI Cells in this library were created in conjunction with the Harvey Mudd College VLSI course, conducted in the Spring of 2007.",This library was originally a part of a 9 week effort involving a team of 30 undergraduate students to build a working R2000-class MIPS Processor. ,"Principal editing conducted by Danny LaValle and Justin Gries, with particular help from Nate Schlossberg, under the direction of ",Professor David Money Harris.,"",The library contains a wide selection of gates in numerous drive strengths.    A 1x gate delivers approximately as much current as a 6-lambda,nMOS transistor.  A pMOS transistor is assumed to deliver half the current of the same sized nMOS transistor.  The gates are sized for minimum,"average delay rather than equal rise and fall delays.  Hence, the average drive strength is about right, but the rising and falling transistions may","be unequal.  This sizing method saves area and power as well as helping speed.  (See CMOS VLSI Design, 3rd Ed., Section6.2.1.6)  A 2x gate",delivers twice the current of a 1x gate.  A _lp gate uses minimum-sized transistors for low power when speed is not a concern.  ,"",The cells are designed with 90 lambda center to center for the power and ground rails.  Rows of cells can be tiled on a 110 lambda pitch with,"room for one metal 3 track between rows, giving room for 11 horizontal metal3 tracks to run over the cells.  The power and ground rails are beefy",enough to carry the current needed for most university-scale chips.,"",Some cells such as multiplexers and flip-flops come in datapath (_dp) and control (_c) flavors.  The datapath flavor factors out inverters to,"go in a zipper at the top of the datapath, saving area within the cell.  The control has integrated inverters for ease of connection.  The flops use",two-phase nonoverlapping clocks so that hold time probelms are easily avoided.  Settable and resettable flops have synchronous reset terminals.,"",The cells are designed using MOSIS Submicron Scalable CMOS design rules.  They are being fabricated in the AMI 0.6 um process using,"lambda = 0.3 um, but are also compatible with the TSMC 180 nm process using labmda = 0.1 um.","","This library may be freely distributed, used, and modified so long as Harvey Mudd College is credited as a source.","","Justin Gries,",HMC Class of '07,Library Team Lead.,"","","","","","",""]
Ngeneric:Facet-Center|art@0||0|0||||AV
X

